<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Monte Albán</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 850 CE (approx.) · capital Monte Albán</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Zapotec capital on a levelled mountain above the Valley of Oaxaca, one of the earliest cities and states of Mesoamerica.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 500 BCE communities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> founded a new centre on a defensible hilltop where the valley's three arms meet. The move, probably by an alliance led from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a>, created a capital that soon dominated the valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Main Plaza was framed by temples, palaces and a ballcourt. The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danzantes">Danzantes</a> carvings show slain or sacrificed captives, and Building J lists conquered places, among the earliest evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization">Zapotec</a> writing and expansion. Monte Albán controlled the valley and areas beyond, kept diplomatic ties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan</a>, and buried its nobles in painted tombs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Monte Albán's population and authority declined between about 700 and 850 CE, and power passed to smaller valley centres such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zaachila">Zaachila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambityeco">Lambityeco</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mitla">Mitla</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtec</a> nobles later reused its tombs, including the famous Tomb 7 treasure.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/monte-alban.jpg" alt="Monte Albán" width="320" height="243"><figcaption>Image: nsaum75 !Dígame¡‎ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Alban_West_Side_Platform.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Monte Albán</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zapotec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Zapotec</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zapotec state, Zapotec state of Monte Alban, Monte Alban</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=175&amp;focus=monte-alban" aria-label="Monte Albán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2049 1545 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Monte Albán lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2369 1801L2368 1804"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2368 1804h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2369 1801h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2369 1801h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=175&amp;focus=monte-alban">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
